---
import { PUBLIC_API_URL } from 'astro:env/server';
import DeveloperPage from '../../components/DeveloperPage.astro';
import { apiOrigin, mcpPath } from '../../lib/developers';
import { pages } from '../../lib/pages';

const api = apiOrigin(PUBLIC_API_URL);
const header = 'Authorization: Bearer shk_...';
---

<DeveloperPage
  page={pages.developerAuthentication}
  heading="Authentication"
  lead="Your systems sign in with an organization API key. AI assistants sign in with the same kind of key, or with OAuth when a person connects them."
>
  <h2>API keys</h2>
  <p>
    An Owner or Admin creates a key in Settings, Integrations, API keys. The key is shown once, so store it as a secret. Send it on
    every request:
  </p>
  <pre tabindex="0"><code>{header}</code></pre>
  <p>
    A key acts for the organization, not for a person, and sees every job. Every change it makes shows in the candidate's activity
    under the key's name. Revoke a key on the same page and it stops working at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Scope</th><th>What it allows</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Read</td><td>Read jobs, applications, candidates, scorecards, templates and reports. Every key has it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Write</td><td>Also change them: create and publish jobs, add applications, move candidates, add notes, tags and tasks.</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Verification read</td>
        <td>
          Read verification results: levels, reasons and evidence. Only an Owner can give it, and every read is recorded on the
          candidate. A level is evidence for a person to review, never a reason to reject anyone on its own.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>OAuth for AI assistants</h2>
  <p>
    A client that signs in only with OAuth, such as a claude.ai custom connector, needs just the server address,{' '}
    <code>{api}{mcpPath}</code>. It finds the authorization server from the first answer, registers itself and sends you to
    ScreenHire to approve it. An Owner or Admin chooses its scopes, never more than they hold themselves. Its access tokens work
    only on the MCP server, and the connection can be ended at once in Settings, Integrations, MCP server.
  </p>

  <h2>Rate limits</h2>
  <p>
    Each key may make 600 requests a minute unless it has a lower limit of its own, counted in fixed one-minute windows. Past it
    the API answers <code>429 Too Many Requests</code>: wait for the next minute and try again. Each MCP tool call is one request.
  </p>

  <h2>When it fails</h2>
  <p>
    A missing, revoked or malformed key gets <code>401</code>. A route the key's scope does not cover gets <code>403</code>. Errors
    are <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457">problem details</a>; the <a href={pages.developerApi.path}>API reference</a>{' '}
    says what each status means.
  </p>
</DeveloperPage>
